from checkpoint import *
D='drinfeldmanin1978selfdual'; M='drinfeldmanin1979instantons'; W='atiyahward1977instantons'; N='narasimhan1962universal'; G='gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'
begin(435)
def it(p,k,t,n,s,f,refs,**kw):
    return item(435,p,k,t,n,s,f,refs,area='Gauge theory and algebraic geometry',**kw)
it('P000738','definition','Curvature and the self-duality equations',r'Manin starts with skew-Hermitian matrix potentials, their curvature, and the equations \(F_{\mu\nu}=\varepsilon^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}F_{\rho\sigma}\).',r'For a unitary connection \(\nabla=d+A\), \(F=dA+A\wedge A\). In an oriented Euclidean four-manifold the self-duality equation is \(F=*F\), or \(F_{\mu\nu}=\frac12\sum_{\rho,\sigma}\varepsilon_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}F_{\rho\sigma}\). The factor is absent only if one sums over a single complementary ordered pair.',r'This is the established differential-geometric setting recalled in \citet[\S1]{'+D+r'}. Its use in the later classification does not make the definition a new 1978 result.',[D],cls='E',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The summation convention is supplied explicitly; the notebook first discusses complementary pairs and then suppresses the convention in its epsilon formula.')
it('P000738','construction','Gauge changes and removable frame singularities',r'Changing a local frame gives \(A\mapsto u^{-1}du+u^{-1}Au\); apparent finite-point singularities are required to disappear in a suitable gauge. The covariant-derivative sum incorrectly repeats \(i\) as its summation index.',r'In a local unitary frame, \(\nabla_\mu s_i=\sum_j A_\mu^{ij}s_j\), with a consistent row or column convention. Smoothness is a property of the connection on the bundle, not of one singular trivialization. Gauge equivalence preserves curvature up to conjugation and preserves self-duality.',r'\citet[\S1]{'+D+r'} formulate regularity and equivalence in terms of bundles with connections. The displayed gauge law is standard; the index repair is elementary and scan-confirmed.',[D],cls='E',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated')
it('P000738-P000739','definition','Regularity at infinity and compactification',r'The connection and its bundle must extend over \(S^4=\mathbb R^4\cup\{\infty\}\); the extended bundle may be nontrivial.',r'A regular instanton here means a smooth unitary self-dual connection on a bundle over the round four-sphere, expressed on the affine chart \(\mathbb R^4\). Extension over infinity is an imposed regularity condition in this talk. It should not be confused with a proof that arbitrary finite-action fields extend.',r'This is precisely the sphere formulation in \citet[\S1]{'+D+r'}. The separate removable-singularity theorem is treated under record 432.',[D],cls='E',literal='correct')
it('P000739','theorem','Clutching classification and charge',r'Clutching along the equator gives \(\pi_3(U(r))=\mathbb Z\), generated by the block embedding of \(SU(2)\), and the sign of charge is reversed with duality.',r'For \(r\geq2\), complex rank-\(r\) bundles over \(S^4\) are classified by an integer clutching class in \(\pi_3(U(r))\), equivalently the second Chern number after fixing orientation. For \(r=1\), \(\pi_3(U(1))=0\). This classifies underlying bundles, not their connections; a fixed charge supports a moduli space of instantons.',r'\citet[\S1 and \S6]{'+D+r'} use the charge \(n\) and rank \(r\) as discrete parameters. The omitted rank-one exception and the distinction from gauge equivalence are essential.',[D],cls='E',match='partial',development='annotated')
it('P000739','construction','Projection from a finite-dimensional trivial bundle',r'The announced construction places \(L\) inside \(H\times S^4\), with \(\dim H=2n+r\), and obtains the connection by differentiating and orthogonally projecting.',r'For the admissible instanton data described below, \(H\times S^4=L\oplus L^\perp\), with \(\operatorname{rank}L=r\), and \(\nabla s=P_L(ds)\). A Hermitian orthogonal projection gives a unitary connection. The sharp ambient dimension and the self-duality conditions belong to the instanton construction, not to an arbitrary connection embedding theorem.',r'\citet[\S3 and \S6]{'+D+r'} give this construction; the announcement was received 27 November 1977, six days after the source date, and published in 1978.',[D],cls='A',literal='correct')
it('P000739','question','Can every connection be induced from a trivial one?',r'A bracketed question asks whether a bundle with connection can always be embedded into a trivial bundle with connection, comparing this with a Euclidean embedding of a Riemannian manifold.',r'For a smooth Hermitian vector bundle over a finite-dimensional smooth manifold and a compatible unitary connection, does there exist a finite-dimensional trivial Hermitian bundle and an isometric bundle embedding for which orthogonal projection of the trivial connection is the prescribed connection?',r'Affirmative before the talk: \citet[p.286, universal-connection theorem and the unitary case]{'+N+r'} announce the joint Narasimhan--Ramanan theorem at the September 1961 Prague symposium; the proceedings appeared in 1962. The universal Stiefel connection gives exactly the projection formulation.',[N],cls='E',literal='correct',problem='GP-1977-25',reconstruction=r'The unitary compatibility and finite-dimensional smooth-base assumptions make the intended projection question precise.')
it('P000739-P000740','construction','Quaternionic real structure and the twistor projection',r'The map \(\sigma(z_1,z_2,z_3,z_4)=(-\bar z_2,\bar z_1,-\bar z_4,\bar z_3)\) has no invariant complex lines; its invariant complex two-planes are identified with \(S^4\). The following page calls \(\sigma\) an involution.',r'On \(\mathbb C^4\), this antilinear map satisfies \(\sigma^2=-1\). It therefore induces a fixed-point-free involution on \(\mathbb{CP}^3\). Its invariant two-planes are quaternionic lines, parametrized by \(\mathbb{HP}^1\simeq S^4\). Sending \([z]\) to \(\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb C}(z,\sigma z)\) is the twistor fibration with fiber \(\mathbb{CP}^1\).',r'The twistor setting is already present in \citet{'+W+r'}; the precise real structure is written in \citet[\S0.2 and \S1.6]{'+M+r'}. The latter is a detailed later exposition, not the first appearance of twistor space.',[W,M],cls='E',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated')
it('P000740','construction','Affine coordinates of the twistor map',r'Two complex quotients give \(x_2+ix_1\) and \(x_4+ix_3\), and a remark says that three coordinates suffice.',r'On \((z_3,z_4)\ne(0,0)\), put \(x_2+ix_1=(z_1\bar z_3+\bar z_2z_4)/(|z_3|^2+|z_4|^2)\) and \(x_4+ix_3=(\bar z_2z_3-z_1\bar z_4)/(|z_3|^2+|z_4|^2)\). These are constant on the complex projective line associated with a quaternionic line. Three complex affine coordinates on \(\mathbb{CP}^3\) suffice; the statement is not a claim that three real coordinates parametrize \(S^4\).',r'\citet[\S1.6]{'+M+r'} print the coordinate construction in the detailed proof. It is an elementary coordinate expression of the earlier twistor map.',[M],cls='E',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
it('P000740','theorem','Self-duality as a type condition on pulled-back curvature',r'The lemma says self-dual two-forms pull back to type \((1,1)\), while three complementary \((2,0)\) components are independent. Corollary (8) omits the component index on its bracket.',r'For the indicated orientation, \(F=*F\) if and only if \((D^*F)^{2,0}=0\); for a unitary connection the conjugate \((0,2)\) condition follows. The three independent components detect the opposite-duality part. The condition \(D^*F=0\) without projection would force \(F=0\), since \(D\) is a surjective submersion.',r'This is the local twistor criterion underlying \citet{'+W+r'} and made explicit in \citet[\S0.2 and \S1.6--1.9]{'+M+r'}. The missing projection is a faithful notebook error.',[W,M],cls='E',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated')
it('P000741','construction','Belavin’s covariant compatibility equations',r'Belavin rewrites the criterion as commuting holomorphic covariant derivatives and then as \([L_1,L_2]=0\) for a spectral parameter \(\lambda\).',r'With \(\nabla_\mu=\partial_\mu+A_\mu\), the pair \(L_1=\lambda(\nabla_1-i\nabla_2)+(\nabla_3+i\nabla_4)\), \(L_2=\lambda(\nabla_3-i\nabla_4)-(\nabla_1+i\nabla_2)\) has zero commutator for all \(\lambda\) exactly when the three self-duality equations hold in this convention. Equivalently the \((1,0)\) part of \(D^*\nabla\) is flat in its holomorphic directions.',r'\citet[\S1.6--1.9]{'+M+r'} use this compatibility and explicitly identify the Belavin--Zakharov route. Here the relative minus sign is retained; it differs from the problematic pair in the earlier Novikov notes.',[M],cls='A',speaker='A. A. Belavin',literal='correct',match='partial')
it('P000741-P000742','construction','From a tensor subspace to the bundle fibers',r'Choose \(I\subset H\otimes\mathbb C^4\), \(\dim I=n\). If \(\ell_1,\ell_2\) cut out a \(\sigma\)-invariant two-plane, take the orthogonal complement of \(I_{\ell_1}+I_{\ell_2}\).',r'Write \(I_\ell=(1\otimes\ell)(I)\subset H\). For a quaternionic line \(D\subset\mathbb C^4\), let \(D^\circ\) be its annihilator and set \(L_D=(\sum_{\ell\in D^\circ}I_\ell)^\perp\). Choosing any basis \(\ell_1,\ell_2\) of \(D^\circ\) gives the same subspace, so the construction is independent of the two defining equations.',r'\citet[\S2--\S3 and the unitary version in \S6]{'+D+r'} supply the linear-algebra construction. The independence is immediate from linearity of contraction.',[D],cls='A',literal='correct')
it('P000742-P000743','theorem','Rank, positivity, and quaternionic orthogonality conditions',r'Conditions 1 and 2 prescribe fiber dimension \(r\) and positivity. Condition 0 is inserted later as \(Q(I_\ell,I_{\ell\circ\sigma})=0\).',r'Require the contraction sum over every quaternionic line to have rank \(2n\), take a positive Hermitian form on \(H\), and impose the quaternionic orthogonality constraint. Since \(\ell\circ\sigma\) is antilinear, the second contraction must use the complex-linear form \(z\mapsto\overline{\ell(\sigma z)}\). With these admissibility conditions the orthogonal complements form a smooth rank-\(r\) bundle and the projected connection is self-dual.',r'The admissibility and projection theorem are in \citet[\S2--\S4 and \S6]{'+D+r'}. The detailed discussion of positivity appears in \citet[\S3--\S4]{'+M+r'}. A bare indefinite form without the full conditions is insufficient.',[D,M],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'Positivity on the ambient Hermitian space is adopted as the convenient modern sufficient hypothesis. The source\textquotesingle s claim that positivity follows from its abbreviated conditions is not independently strengthened here.')
it('P000742-P000743','theorem','Completeness and the equivalence relation on instanton data',r'Theorem 1 says that the construction gives all self-dual Yang--Mills solutions and that nonisomorphic bundles correspond to inequivalent solutions. Isomorphisms of tensor diagrams are then described.',r'Every regular unitary instanton on \(S^4\) is obtained from admissible finite-dimensional data. Two data sets yield gauge-equivalent connections precisely under the corresponding isomorphisms of diagrams, including an isometry on \(H\) and a change of basis in \(I\). The classification concerns bundles with connection; nonisomorphism of the underlying topological bundle is far too coarse.',r'Exactly the completeness and uniqueness theorem of \citet[\S4]{'+D+r'}, received 27 November 1977 and published in 1978; \citet[\S3]{'+M+r'} provide the fuller proof. The upper tensor factor in the notebook diagram needs \(\mathbb C^4\).',[D,M],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1977-24',development='annotated')
it('P000743','remark','The quadruple-of-subspaces intervention',r'Gelfand invokes unpublished work with Ponomarev on quadruples with an automorphism. Ponomarev takes the four images of \(H\) in \((H^{\oplus4})/I\), then asks for the quadruples compatible with an orthogonal-complement operation.',r'The quotient construction indeed associates four subspaces to a tensor inclusion. But the notes do not specify the additional automorphism, nondegeneracy hypotheses, duality functor or equivalence relation needed for the claimed Hermitian classification; no exact classification theorem can be reconstructed.',r'\citet{'+G+r'} is the earlier complex quadruple-classification lead, received 21 October 1970 and published in 1971. Its metadata do not verify the additional unpublished Hermitian assertion. The instanton diagram theorem of \citet[\S4]{'+D+r'} solves its own specified classification, not every possible quadruple-with-duality problem.',[G,D],cls='F',speaker='I. M. Gelfand and V. A. Ponomarev',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='low',status='As of 10 November 2026, the extra unpublished assertion remains unidentified; the earlier quadruple work is thematic evidence only.')
it('P000743','construction','Gram matrices and the absence of horizontal sections',r'A basis of \(I\) produces \(4n\) vectors \(e_{ij}\in H\) and their Gram matrix \(\widetilde Q\). The assumption \(\operatorname{rank}\widetilde Q=2n+r\) is equated with absence of horizontal sections.',r'For \(i=1,\ldots,n\), write the basis tensor as \(e_i=\sum_{j=1}^4e_{ij}\otimes f_j\). The Gram matrix determines these vectors up to unitary equivalence on their span. Full rank \(2n+r\) means they span \(H\). For the instanton data, failure of spanning gives a constant orthogonal summand and hence a flat factor; the converse uses the admissibility theorem.',r'\citet[\S6--\S7]{'+D+r'} state the full-span criterion and the horizontal-section interpretation. The notebook\textquotesingle s basis endpoint \(e_r\) must be \(e_n\).',[D],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated')
it('P000744','construction','The constrained four-by-four Gram blocks',r'Every block has the displayed six-parameter pattern; Hermitian symmetry and rank are required, while Conditions 1 and 2 are expressly omitted.',r'The block pattern is \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a&0&c&d\\0&a&e&f\\f&-d&b&0\\-e&c&0&b\end{smallmatrix}\right)\). It expresses the quaternionic orthogonality equations. Global Hermitian symmetry relates the block at \((k,\ell)\) to the adjoint of the block at \((\ell,k)\). Positivity and the pointwise rank conditions remain necessary; the linear block pattern alone does not classify regular instantons.',r'\citet[\S6]{'+D+r'} give the Gram-matrix formulation and explicitly acknowledge further regularity inequalities. Thus the notebook is an announcement of constraints, not an assertion that its listed linear equations suffice.',[D],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
it('P000744','construction','Affine generators of the orthogonal complement',r'The vectors \(h_{k1}\) and \(h_{k2}\) are called \(2n\) global generators of \(L^\perp\) over \(S^4\), although their formulas use affine coordinates.',r'On the chart \(\mathbb R^4\), the vectors \(h_{k1}=(x_2+ix_1)e_{k1}+(x_4-ix_3)e_{k2}+e_{k3}\) and \(h_{k2}=-(x_4+ix_3)e_{k1}+(x_2-ix_1)e_{k2}+e_{k4}\) span \(L_x^\perp\). They are not a global frame on \(S^4\) for nonzero charge: a rank-\(2n\) bundle globally generated by exactly \(2n\) independent sections would be trivial, contradicting \(c(L)c(L^\perp)=1\).',r'\citet[\S6]{'+D+r'} use these vectors in affine Euclidean coordinates. The local construction extends geometrically across infinity after changing trivialization, not by retaining one global affine frame.',[D],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated')
it('P000744-P000745','method','Computing the connection from a kernel and orthonormal frame',r'Solve \(2n\) homogeneous equations in \(2n+r\) variables, choose a fundamental system, orthonormalize, and differentiate to obtain \(A_\mu^{ij}=Q(\partial_\mu y^{(i)},y^{(j)})\).',r'On a chart where a chosen minor is nonzero, solve \(Q(v,h_{ka}(x))=0\), \(k=1,\ldots,n\), \(a=1,2\), for a local basis of \(L_x\). Its coefficients are rational functions of the real coordinates. Gram--Schmidt introduces square roots of positive rational functions. If \(Y\) is the resulting orthonormal column frame, the connection matrix is \(Y^*dY\), up to the notebook\textquotesingle s transposed frame convention.',r'\citet[\S6]{'+D+r'} describe this explicit algorithm. Its formula is local and uses nonvanishing minors; no global rational orthonormal frame is implied.',[D],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact')
it('P000745','theorem','The cohomological monad classification',r'The final geometric theorem says diagrams classify bundles on \(\mathbb{CP}^3\) satisfying \(H^i(L(-2))=0\), the same for \(L^*\), and \(H^2(L(-1))=H^0(L(-1))=0\); the range of \(i\) is not stated.',r'A precise published version defines an admissible locally free sheaf \(E\) by \(H^i(E(k))=0\) for \(i=0,1\) with \(i+k\leq-1\), and for \(i=2,3\) with \(i+k\geq0\). The middle cohomology of a special monad \(0\to I\otimes\mathcal O(-1)\to H\otimes\mathcal O\to I^{\prime}\otimes\mathcal O(1)\to0\), with fiberwise injective and surjective end maps, gives an equivalence with admissible sheaves. Instantons additionally require the real and positivity conditions.',r'\citet[\S0.3--0.4 and Theorem~2.1]{'+M+r'} prove this equivalence; the paper was received 26 May 1978 and published in 1979. The short notebook vanishings are a precursor, not an independently complete list with all quantifiers.',[M],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The full admissibility definition and monad exactness conditions are taken from the later theorem and are explicitly separated from the notebook\textquotesingle s abbreviated cohomology conditions.')
prob('GP-1977-25','Inducing a unitary connection by orthogonal projection',r'Given a smooth Hermitian vector bundle with a compatible connection over a finite-dimensional smooth manifold, realize it isometrically inside a finite-dimensional trivial Hermitian bundle so that its connection is the orthogonal projection of the trivial connection.',['1977-435.6'],r'Affirmative before the seminar: the Narasimhan--Ramanan universal-connection theorem was announced in 1961 and is stated in the 1962 Prague proceedings. As of 10 November 2026, this is an established theorem, not an open problem.',[N])
